.TH CHMOD 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
chmod \- change file modes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chmod
.I mode file
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chmod
changes the file permission bits of each
.I file
operand to
.IR mode .
Multiple
.I file
operands are processed in turn; a failure changing one file's mode
does not stop the rest from being attempted. The
.I mode
operand itself, however, is parsed once, before any file is touched;
if it is malformed,
.B chmod
reports it as a usage error and does not attempt any of the
.I file
operands at all.
.PP
Each
.I file
is
.BR stat (2)'d
before
.I mode
is applied to it, so a symbolic
.I mode
(see OPERANDS below) is always evaluated relative to that specific
file's own current permission bits \(em the same
.I mode
string can change different files by different amounts depending on
each one's starting permissions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Not implemented. When given as the first argument,
.B chmod
prints a diagnostic and exits immediately with status 1 rather than
recurse (or silently fail to recurse) into a directory tree. Only this
exact, unadorned first argument is recognized this way; only a
single-file, non-recursive change of mode is supported.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I mode
The change to make to each
.IR file 's
mode, in one of two forms:
.RS
.TP
Octal
One to four octal digits (characters
.BR 0 \- 7 ,
value at most
.BR 07777 ),
taken as the resulting mode bits directly \(em this is the only form
that can set the setuid, setgid, or sticky bits.
.TP
Symbolic
A comma-separated list of clauses, each of the form
\fI[who...](op perm...)+\fR, where
.I who
is any combination of the letters
.BR u ", " g ", " o ", and " a
.RB ( a
means
.BR ugo ,
and is assumed when
.I who
is omitted),
.I op
is one of
.BR + ", " \- ", or " = ,
and
.I perm
is any combination of the letters
.BR r ", " w ", and " x .
When
.I who
is omitted from a clause, the
.I perm
bits that clause would set are first filtered by the invoking
process's file creation mask, one class at a time; when
.I who
is given explicitly, no such filtering is applied. A
.I mode
operand may begin with
.BR + ", " \- ", or " =
directly (with
.I who
therefore omitted), so, for example,
.B chmod \-w file
is a valid symbolic
.I mode
that removes write permission, not an unrecognized option.
.RE
.IP
The
.BR X ", " s ", and " t
.I perm
letters, and using a
.I who
letter itself as a
.I perm
to copy another class's bits, are not supported in symbolic
.IR mode \|;
a symbolic
.I mode
using any of them is rejected outright as invalid rather than
approximated. Use an octal
.I mode
to set the setuid, setgid, or sticky bits.
.TP
.I file
A pathname of a file whose permission bits are to be changed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The utility executed successfully and all requested changes were made.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: an invalid
.I mode
operand (no files are changed in this case),
.BR \-R ,
a missing operand, or a failure changing the mode of one or more of
the named files.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mkfifo (1)
